He stood at the copying table in the empty wing, with the shelf above it at the edge of his sight and the rain loud on the long windows, and said them aloud, once, at the pace she had used, so that the room would have heard them if the room had been listening.

*What you carry is worth what it has not done.*

*A thing kept is a wall. A thing spent is a door, and doors open both ways.*

*The keeper is invisible. The witness is a location.*

*Nobody has ever protected a man by believing him.*

Then he stood there and listened to them, the way you listen to a bell after it has been struck, to hear whether it is cracked.

What he carried had done nothing for twelve years. It was worth exactly that. He had always thought the line meant that a thing unused kept its whole value, like a coin in a drawer. Standing there he heard what it actually said. It said the value was the not-doing. It had been worth twelve years of nothing, and it was worth nothing still.

A thing kept is a wall. It had been a wall. It had stood between what he knew and whoever would want to know he knew it, and it had kept him safe, perfectly, every day. But it was a wall round the case, not round the boy. The boy was on the other side of it with the rest of the weather.

The keeper is invisible. He had been invisible for fifteen years. The boy's people had never been invisible for a day. All year they had walked about the hill in the open and let themselves be counted, the girl on the walls with her chalk and the big one at the frames and the researcher with her lamp lit in the top window every night where the whole valley could see it. Five days ago, at the gate, the house itself had stood in the middle of its own court and read the paper out loud. Nobody on that side had hidden anything. They had simply been there.

The witness is a location. He had always heard that as a warning: a man who has seen a thing is only a place where the thing can be found, so do not be found. Now, in the empty wing, it sounded like a choice. He would rather be the place the boy could find.

And the last line.

He had believed the last line more than the other three together. It was the one that had kept him clean. You did not protect a subject by believing him; you protected him by counting what he did, and filing it true, and letting the true thing be your only argument. He had never believed anybody in his life. He had counted them.

He had counted the boy for a year. Two slips on a common-room fire. A season of figures that never once settled into a line. An afternoon at Norhold where everything held back went out in one breath, on purpose, in front of eight thousand people. He had counted all of it, and filed none of it, and somewhere in the counting, without asking his leave, it had turned into the other thing.

He believed him.

*Nobody has ever protected a man by believing him.* It was true. He had never seen it done. He was going to try.

The wing's clock went on with its slow tick over the counter. Nothing in the room changed its note. Seln stood with his two hands flat on the copying table and understood, quite calmly, that this was the largest thing that had happened to him since a green corridor twelve years ago, and that it had happened on a weekday morning between the post and the noon bell, and that nobody would ever write it down.

He reached up and took the case off the shelf.


He sat down on the lip of the south bank above the hollow, where she had come out of the shadow, because his legs had decided that for him. The chalk was cold through his clothes. Below him on the river side the hill fell away through hawthorn and willow to the brown water, and he could see a long way down the valley toward the city, the flat land shining with floodwater in long strips, and a smudge of smoke on the far edge of it that was Ostrand.

It had been waiting for him since he held still on his knees, as it always waited. It came to the place behind his eyes that belonged to nothing else, and stayed there, patient as a clerk, until he had a moment to look. He looked now.

```
FRAGMENT ACQUIRED
[unnamed] — Anchor-adjacent. Duration: sustained. Integration: partial.
Tier equivalent: Silver.
Note: fixed-point binding component; lattice-perception component.
Contact-to-short range.
Acquisition: directed. Engagement: adversarial, combat.
```

He read it through twice. Then he counted, because he could not help it.

Eight.

*Silver*, it said. It was the first notice that had ever said Silver. The guess on the leaf had been right; he had priced it at the weight it came at. *Anchor-adjacent*, it said, and he thought of Seln at the long table with his eyes shut, saying the name, and Karis counting the words off on her fingers, keeping them straight. *Fixed-point binding.* That was the thing against his coat, and the thing he had let go of. *Lattice-perception.* That was the grey net he had seen laid down the whole cut, which he could not see now at all. *Contact-to-short range.* Close to her, cheap. Further off, at a price he had watched her pay, half a second at a time.

"The off-side one." Seln looked at it as if it were a thing he had found on a beach. "I was under the wagon when they came back down the gully. They walked past it. Nobody looked under it. Nobody looks under a wagon." He turned and dropped the pin into the ditch on the north side, where it went into the mud without a sound and did not come up. "I didn't see anything up here. The bank was in the way. I heard it."

He did not ask what he had heard. He did not ask how it had ended. He looked at Cael on the bank for perhaps one breath longer than he looked at anything else.

"Ribs?" he said.

"Right side."

"Then sit there. Don't lift anything."

He went down the road to help the coachman with the near horse.

Cael watched him go. All morning, from the ferry landing to now, Seln had not been in a single place where anybody on the road could see him. He had not been in a single place where the team could see him. He had not been in a single place where Cael could see him either, and Cael had looked, once, during the third exchange, down the bank toward the willows, and found nothing but willows. The six had come to this road expecting five people and a coachman. They had counted four. The fifth had been underneath their wagon with his hand on its pin.

---

He took Hesk's book out of his coat.

The leather was warm from his body. There was a small mark on the cover, over the place where the book lay against his ribs, a faint dull patch in the grain as if a thumb had pressed there for a long time. He looked at it and knew what had made it. He did not try to rub it out.

He took Hesk's book out of his coat and put it in the pocket.

It fitted. It went in all the way down without being pushed, and stood there, with a quarter-inch of leather above it on every side, and the flap came down over it and the buckle went home. Hesk had never seen the book since he bound it. He had bound it from memory, cut the pocket from memory, at the far end of the country, and it fitted to a hair.

There was a note folded small under the flap. It was on a leaf from Hesk's order-pad, in Hesk's small fast hand, with one initial at the foot. He read it to himself first. Then he read it again.

*Eighteen. A man's tools should fit the road he's actually on, not the one they promised him. Your grandmother would say I'm being unsubtle. She'd be right. Mind the shape. — H.*

He did not read it aloud. Nobody asked him to. He folded it small again and put it in the satchel's pocket with the book, against the book's back board, and buckled the flap.

Across the table Seln was looking at the satchel. He had not looked at anything else since the brown paper came off. He did not say anything about it. He looked at the strap that went across the body, and the buckle that would not catch, and the flap that came down over everything, and Cael thought, though he could not have said how he knew it, that Seln was thinking about roads.


*Eighteen. The eleventh. Hesk's satchel on the sill. The book fits it.*

*What I carry: eight confirmed.*

*Wind-adjacent — Lira, the burst. Pressure-adjacent — the Bronze from Ardenmere, the push. Iron-adjacent — Brom, the read, which found the fourteen. Compression-adjacent — Reydan, the give. Ember-adjacent — Karis, the spark, two contacts and no more. Shadow-adjacent — the stair, sealed, rent from waking. Storm-adjacent — Daeva, the lane; moved twice this week, forty minutes and a quarter hour, and settled. Anchor-adjacent — the hand with no face; idle by naming; wants doorways.*

*Under all eight, the still place I don't count.*

*Still open. Still real. Patience.*

He looked at the list for a long time. Eight lines and a ninth that was not a line. Seven names he could have written a letter to, and one he never could.

Somebody knocked at the door.


